text:paper:1662880 {"content":"How to Write a Good Scientific Paper: Review Articles\n\nThis PDF file contains the editorial “How to Write a Good Scientific Paper: Review Articles” for JM3 Vol. 15 Issue 02","paper_id":1662880}
text:paper:1581572 {"content":"How to Write a Good Scientific Paper: Picking the Right Journal\n\nThis PDF file contains the editorial “How to Write a Good Scientific Paper: Picking the Right Journal” for JM3 Vol. 14 Issue 04","paper_id":1581572}
text:paper:1607155 {"content":"How to Write a Good Scientific Paper: Citations\n\nThis is the second in a planned series of editorials covering all aspects of good science writing.","paper_id":1607155}
text:paper:1603032 {"content":"How to Write a Good Scientific Paper: Significance\n\nA paper must meet four criteria before it is publishable in a scientific journal.  \n•The content of the paper must match the scope of the journal\n•The quality of the paper (method and execution of the research, as well as the writing) must be sufficiently high\n•It must present novel results (with the exception of review papers and the like)\n•The results must be significant enough to be worth reading about (and thus worth publishing).\n\nAfter a quick review of the first three items, I’d like to spend some time talking about the last item, paper significance.","paper_id":1603032}
text:paper:1529470 {"content":"Theoretical and experimental problems at IPhO 42 in Thailand\n\nThis paper presents both the theoretical (without solutions) and the experimental problems for the 42nd International Physics Olympiad (IPhO 42) organized in Bangkok, Thailand, 10–18 July 2012. Experimental problems on electrical and mechanical black boxes are given with detailed solutions. The performance of the contestants in the theoretical and the experimental sections is compared.","paper_id":1529470}
text:paper:1534594:overlay {"content":"Exact solution of the Dirac equation with a linear potential under the influence of the generalized uncertainty principle\n\nWe present an effective method of exact solution of the (1+1)-dimensional Dirac equation with a linear scalar-like potential in the context of the generalized uncertainty principle (GUP). The effectiveness of the method depends on knowledge of the energy spectrum of the harmonic oscillator with GUP. We employ the property of the harmonic oscillator that the kinetic energy part of the Hamiltonian is of equal weight to the potential energy part to obtain the energy levels from the deformation of the Dirac equation with a scalar-like linear potential through the generalized Heisenberg algebra of a GUP. Our result fully coincides with the results found in the literature. This paper thus provides an easier, alternative and effective method of exact solution of the Dirac equation with a linear potential with GUP.","paper_id":1534594}
text:paper:1226525:overlay {"content":"On the (2+1)-dimensional Dirac equation in a constant magnetic field with a minimal length uncertainty\n\nWe exactly solve the (2+1)-dimensional Dirac equation in a constant magnetic field in the presence of a minimal length. Using a proper ansatz for the wave function, we transform the Dirac Hamiltonian into two 2-dimensional non-relativistic harmonic oscillator and obtain the solutions without directly solving the corresponding differential equations which are presented by Menculini et al. [Phys. Rev. D 87, 065017 (2013)]. We also show that Menculini et al. solution is a subset of the general solution which is related to the even quantum numbers.","paper_id":1226525}
text:paper:1606518:overlay {"content":"Minimal length Dirac equation revisited\n\nWe will show that the Dirac equation under the assumption of a generalized uncertainty principle corresponds to a Schrodinger-like equation with a new effective potential. Next, as typical examples, the potential well and the step potential are studied in an exact analytical manner. The scattering problems as well as the reflection and transmission parameters are reported.","paper_id":1606518}
